			<orm:ModelNote id="_FAF6D2C1-76ED-41D9-A440-BA8BAD404B58">
				<orm:Text>PUS CR: raise CR asking to add a note recommending to use the same ID for both Source and Destination on-board. implying that the ground application process uses other application process (user) ids than 0..2047</orm:Text>
			</orm:ModelNote>
			<orm:ModelNote id="_D14830C4-2D0D-4FA5-BE29-554747806146">
				<orm:Text>Interfaced system Objects - System objects</orm:Text>
			</orm:ModelNote>
			<orm:ModelNote id="_215DE182-488D-45A5-BA99-403A72EB2CD6">
				<orm:Text>The equality constraint enforces 5.4.3.2.d, 
but the verbalization of the requirements is different from the one in the standard.</orm:Text>
				<orm:ReferencedBy>
					<orm:SetComparisonConstraint ref="_6E5F933D-4B65-4212-BBE4-A24E3121BCDF" />
				</orm:ReferencedBy>
			</orm:ModelNote>
			<orm:ModelNote id="_65F8D595-C60D-4280-B65C-F1B9BB2D6C3D">
				<orm:Text>the on-board parameter is defined as the lowest level of elementary data item on-board,
 such as a measurement from a sensor, actuations computed by the Guidance and Navigation Control, or the current state of a software process.
Note: in case of cold redundancy, the nominal and redundant sensor may use the same onboard parameter ID. for hot redundancy, each one uses its own parameter id. Usually, a given onboard parameter can be accessed by several apid (since it is stored in a single data pool)
It may also happen that a low level PUS compliant unit is in hot or cold redundancy and controlled via two different APID (e.g. star trackers). If hot redundancy, the structures of e.g. TM packets are the same but the onboard parameters are different. If cold redundancy, they may be the same.</orm:Text>
			</orm:ModelNote>
			<orm:ModelNote id="_CD5E4ADF-F4C3-4F05-B344-B176F6E73F5E">
				<orm:Text>The object path is the combination of the repository path and the object name.</orm:Text>
